~ core.test — the test harness and its reporter. Import with `<< core.test`.
~ Reference: docs/corelib/test.md.
~
~ ── Assertions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ `assert` and `expect` are provided by the COMPILER, so they need no import and are not
~ defined here. Both take the value under test and a matcher:
~
~   assert(2 + 2, equals(4))    report at YOUR call site and exit 101 on failure
~   expect(2 + 2, equals(4))    report, mark the case failed, and carry on (tests only)
~
~ The matchers are `equals`, `contains`, `not`, `isOk`, `isNotOk` — see
~ docs/corelib/test.md.
~
~   failAt(message)       fail outright at the CALLER's location, the way `assert` does —
~                         what a check of your own is built from. Take a trailing
~                         `site :: Site` and forward it to report ITS caller:
~                           assertEven = (n :: Num, site :: Site) -> $ =>
~                             n % 2 == 0 ? $ : failAt("`n` is odd", site)
~
~ ── The harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ `quilon test` runs a file's top-level `describe` blocks; every other command compiles
~ the file without them. A test file has no `^`. A failing `expect` skips the rest of its
~ case, so a run reports every case — see docs/corelib/test.md.
~
~   describe(name, body)  group of cases (nestable). `body` is a `() -> $` closure.
~   it(name, body)        one case, reported once it has run.
~
~   describe("trimming", () => <
~     it("drops both ends", () => expect("  x  ".trim(), equals("x")))
~   >)
~
~ ── Reporters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ What a run looks like is decided here, not in the compiler: the harness records what
~ happened through the reporter-agnostic registry (`__test_*`) and renders through the
~ `report*` functions below. A reporter of your own defines the same three, and the
~ entry `quilon test` synthesizes calls `reportSummary()` last, for the exit code.
<< core.io

~ Report `message` at `site` — the location of the call that left the `site` argument off —
~ in the compiler's diagnostic frame, then exit 101.
>> failAt = (message :: Text, site :: Site) -> $ => <
  color = __color_enabled(stderr)
  position = color ? "\e[36m" : ""
  problem = color ? "\e[1;31m" : ""
  frame = color ? "\e[2m" : ""
  plain = color ? "\e[0m" : ""

  number = "`site.line`"
  gutter = " ".repeat(number.length)
  lead = " ".repeat(site.column - 1)
  carets = "^".repeat(site.width)

  ~ Keep in step with `shorten_path` in the runtime, which shortens a path the same way.
  room = 60
  file = site.file.length > room
    ? "…" + site.file.slice(site.file.length - room + 1, site.file.length)
    : site.file

  eprint("`position``file`:`site.line`:`site.column`:`plain`")
  eprint("`problem``message``plain`")
  eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain`")
  eprint("`frame``number` |`plain` `site.excerpt`")
  eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain` `lead``problem``carets``plain`")
  __exit(101)
>

~ ── The default reporter ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

~ Two spaces per level of `describe` nesting.
>> indent = (depth :: Num) -> Text => "  ".repeat(depth)

~ `text` in green — plain when stdout is not a terminal that wants color.
>> green = (text :: Text) -> Text =>
  __color_enabled(stdout) ? "\e[32m`text`\e[0m" : text

~ `text` in red, on the same terms as `green`.
>> red = (text :: Text) -> Text =>
  __color_enabled(stdout) ? "\e[31m`text`\e[0m" : text

~ A `describe` group was entered, at `depth` (1 for an outermost group).
>> reportSuite = (name :: Text, depth :: Num) -> $ => print("`indent(depth - 1)``name`")

~ A case ran, at `depth`; `failed` says whether an `expect` in it failed.
>> reportCase = (name :: Text, depth :: Num, failed :: Bool) -> $ => <
  mark = failed ? red("✗") : green("✓")
  print("`indent(depth)``mark` `name`")
>

~ The run's total, and its exit code — non-zero when any case failed. The synthesized
~ entry point ends with this call, so what it returns IS the process status.
>> reportSummary = () -> Num => <
  passed = __test_passed()
  failed = __test_failed()
  tally = "`passed` passed, `failed` failed"
  print("")
  print(failed == 0 ? green(tally) : red(tally))
  failed == 0 ? 0 : 1
>

~ ── describe / it ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

~ A group of cases, nestable. `body` runs immediately; the group is only there to name
~ and indent what it contains.
>> describe = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  depth = __test_suite_enter()
  reportSuite(name, depth)
  body()
  __test_suite_leave()
  $
>

~ One case, reported once `body` has run. A failing `expect` inside `body` marks the case
~ and skips what is left of it, so the report says which way the case went and the run
~ continues with the next one.
>> it = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  body()
  failed = __test_case_failing() == 1
  reportCase(name, __test_case_finish(), failed)
>
